          "en": "Supreme People's Court\non Whether, When a Suspended Sentence Is Revoked, the Time during Which the Offender Was Held in Custody\nbefore the Suspended Sentence Was Pronounced May Be Set Off against the Term of the Sentence: Reply\nFa Shi No. 11 [2002]\n(Adopted at the 1,220th meeting of the Judicial Committee of the Supreme People's Court on 8 April 2002; promulgated by announcement of the Supreme People's Court on 10 April 2002; coming into force on 18 April 2002)\nTo the higher people's courts of all provinces, autonomous regions and municipalities directly under the Central Government, the Military Court of the Chinese People's Liberation Army, and the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the Higher People's Court of the Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region:\nRecently, some courts have reported that it is unclear whether, when a suspended sentence is revoked, the time during which an offender was held in custody before the suspended sentence was pronounced may be set off against the term of the sentence. After study, the following reply is given:\nIn accordance with Article 77 of the Criminal Law, where the suspended sentence pronounced on a criminal is revoked and the punishment originally imposed is executed, the time during which he was held in custody before the suspended sentence was pronounced shall be set off against the term of the sentence."
